  "summary": "A style of painting practiced by scholar-officials in China emphasizing personal expression and scholarly themes.",
  "description": "Characterized by brushwork that suggests rather than depicts, with landscapes and natural imagery serving as metaphors for the artist's inner world. Developed as a counterpoint to professional court painting, valuing spontaneity, individual expression, and literary references over technical perfection.",
  "culturalContext": "Although the term itself originates much later, the artists later attached to it go back to at least the Tang dynasty, with a larger group from (rather confusingly) the Northern Song dynasty.  In the latter period the tradition of literati landscape painting seems to have acquired most of the characteristics that it kept throughout its history, and a number of writings on theory have survived from it; the extent to which actual works by Tang or Song masters have survived is still a matter of controversy.  Several of the famous names from the periods between the Tang and Song periods were very high officials, but in later periods landscape painting became a refuge and form of muted protest within their class for officials out of favour, or who opposed and avoided the court.",
